WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.710
- It's not your eyes

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.510
or the brightness of your screen.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.740
This is a very dark episode
of HBO's "House of the Dragon"

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.970
on a $650 TCL LCD television.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.380
What are we even looking at?

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.180
This is that same scene
on a $3,000 OLED Sony TV.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.280
Better, kind of.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.600
At least you can make out this person

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.200
and these blades of grass but still.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.260
HBO's hit show looks like
it was afforded everything

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.093
but a lamp.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:31.530
In the world of entertainment,

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.180
there's a dark TV problem

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:36.060
and HBO's "House of the
Dragon" isn't the only culprit.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.580
It just happens to be the most recent.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.620
For years, viewers have criticized many

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.130
of television's biggest shows
for being too hard to see.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.330
- [Viewer] Why did that episode

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.187
of "House of the Dragon" look so bad?

00:00:48.187 --> 00:00:50.400
"Stranger Things" season four, too dark.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.690
I can barely see it.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.090
- If your intentional creative decision

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:58.290
is that people can't see the
TV show that you're making,

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.507
then you're making a podcast, all right?

00:01:00.507 --> 00:01:01.340
(audience chuckling)

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:02.430
- So why is this happening?

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.080
Is it the way the shows are being filmed?

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.670
Is it your TV settings?

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.680
Or is it the TVs themselves?

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.740
Turns out, it's a combination
of all those things.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.780
To better understand why
things appear so dark,

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.070
I gathered a lot of TVs

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:15.630
and recruited Kevin Miller.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.200
- So I'm a calibrator, both
of professional companies,

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.630
post-production facilities in Manhattan.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.690
- [Kenny] Kevin is serious
about TV display settings.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.490
He adjusts them so screens show exactly

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.830
what the creators of shows intended.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:31.710
- I calibrate TVs like
this in people's homes.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.900
- [Kenny] To Kevin, it's not
about how the shows are shot,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.730
it's all about the TVs.

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:37.050
What's his argument?

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.190
- It's not the content

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:40.770
because the content is
an artistic decision

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:41.760
made by the director.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.230
- [Kenny] HBO responded similarly

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.637
after the Driftmark "House
of the Dragon" episode.

00:01:45.637 --> 00:01:47.760
"House of the Dragon"
showrunner, Ryan Condal,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.070
pointed a finger at the
streaming services themselves

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.600
for some of the issues.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.850
Sure, but if we can't
see the creator's work,

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.620
why even watch?

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.030
Kevin and other experts I talked

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.010
to attribute the poor viewing experience

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.140
to technical issues on your TV.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.120
Here's how you may be
able to fix some of them

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.060
and at least see some
of those darker scenes.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.860
The first reason you might
be experiencing issues

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.080
with those dark scenes: your TV.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.270
More specifically, its screen.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:15.060
- OLED has the best
black level reproduction

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.040
of any display device ever made

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.220
and that's because it's
an emissive technology.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.800
A light gets shot off to create black.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.920
Look at the difference
between a $2,200 OLED TV

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.270
and a $650 LCD with LED
back-lit technology.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.730
Both are using default settings.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.190
This, of course, is an
extreme example meant

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.270
to demonstrate the difference

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:39.450
in how OLED and LCDs display blacks.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.700
Higher-end LCDs can do a better job

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.048
than what you are seeing here.

00:02:43.048 --> 00:02:43.890
(screen beeping)

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:47.040
And to be fair, the camera
can't reproduce exactly

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.150
what we saw.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.740
But if you're in the market for a new TV

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.480
and wanna see every shade of black

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:52.890
in a variety of environments,

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:54.510
it's hard to go wrong with an OLED.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.060
Just prepared to spend more money.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.220
But don't worry, you
don't necessarily need

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.050
to buy an expensive new TV.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.220
Do you have a light switch?

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.810
- Any ambient light in a room

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:06.810
will obscure shadow
detail in dark material,

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:10.440
even a light-colored surface
like the wall if it's white

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.260
will reflect light from a display.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.270
- [Kenny] The fix is relatively easy.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.120
You've gotta go into
full Dracula viewing mode

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.170
and get as close to creating

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.690
your own home movie theater as possible.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:22.860
In an interview with Deadline,

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.270
the "House of the Dragon" showrunner said

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.560
the show is calibrated

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.750
with that sort of environment in mind.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.480
Also, make sure you're
viewing your TV head on

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.780
or within about 45 degrees to either side.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:34.920
Then there's picture settings,

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.040
most often found hidden in
one of your TV's submenus.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.300
- The first thing you wanna find

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.120
is the most accurate best picture mode.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.340
- [Kenny] Yes, purists
like Kevin will direct you

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.930
to TV settings made for film,

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.500
like this Sony TV custom
Dolby Vision Picture Profile

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.130
or Filmmaker Mode but
they will appear darker.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.420
If you wanna lighten everything up,

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:55.890
try looking for bright presets

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:57.510
or using the Vivid Profile,

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.190
which is available on most TVs,

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:02.100
including Sony, LG and TCL.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.150
And on Samsung units,

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.530
the dynamic setting is tuned

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.350
for brighter, more highly
lit viewing environments.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.390
One of the many tools
Kevin uses to calibrate

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.970
requires a Blu-ray player
and a special disc.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:13.590
But because this is 2022

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.289
and few people have time for that,

00:04:15.289 --> 00:04:17.400
(door creaking)

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.380
you can use a calibration tool on YouTube

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.370
though a purist like Kevin

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.560
would never be caught dead using one.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.030
- No, definitely not.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.470
Nothing against YouTube.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.450
- Find one of these black
level tests on YouTube,

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.730
load it up in full screen
and adjust your brightness

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.370
or black level until you
can just barely see the box

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.080
next to the reference black.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.690
Then lastly, if you're still
unsatisfied with how dark some

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.900
of these shows are, you
can try turning off HDR.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.360
In my testing, the SDR version
of "House of the Dragon"

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.720
was considerable brighter
and easier to view.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:46.890
On most streaming devices,

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:49.080
including Apple TV and Google Chromecast,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.590
the HDR toggle is found
in the settings menu.

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:56.580
So is this issue likely
to go away any time soon?

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:57.840
Probably not.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.820
Now that showrunners are
making television shows

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.140
that feel like movies,

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.900
they're also giving them the
same cinematic treatment,

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.850
despite the fact that
we're just watching them

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:06.990
in our living rooms.

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:09.210
Fortunately, "House of
the Dragon" season two

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:10.860
hasn't even begun filming yet,

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:11.970
so you've got lots of time

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.283
to prepare your TV
viewing for next season.

